  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM EDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nSurface high pressure building across the Midwest and eventually \ninto the Great Lakes later tonight will yield mainly dry conditions \nthrough Friday. The cold front that was supposed to come through \nyesterday slowed quite a bit from what was originally anticipated \nand stalled just to our northwest this morning; as that front now \nmakes its way through our forecast area today, an area of showers is \npassing through north-central Indiana as seen on current radar. \nWhile I can't rule out an isolated storm, a tight moisture gradient \nin the low levels and SBCAPE of 1000 J/kg or less should keep \nwidespread chances at bay. Highs in the upper 70s are expected \ntoday. It will be noticeably less humid with dewpoints only in \nthe 50s once the front passes through and northwest flow ensues \non the backside, ushering in much drier air tonight.\n\nMuch like last night, patchy dense fog is possible given clear skies \nand light winds. Should radiation fog occur, visibilities should not \nbe as low as they were this morning given a lack of saturation at \nthe surface. I would expect that areas south of US 24 would be most \nprimed for fog to develop today given the showers currently moving \nthrough. Friday will be a fantastic day with high pressure overhead \nproviding low humidity, highs near 80 degrees, and lots of sunshine!\n\nOur next chances for rain and storms arrive starting before daybreak \nSaturday and continuing, especially south of US 24 once again, \nthroughout the day Saturday. Far northwest Indiana and southwest \nLower Michigan may stay entirely dry given that the better dynamics \nfor this rain/storm potential will be to our southeast. A warm front \nwill lift northward Saturday, and with a developing upper level \ntrough over the Great Lakes, scattered showers and storms appear \nincreasingly likely for our southeast half. There is some \nuncertainty on how far north and west the rain will get, with our \nhighest PoPs of about 40-60% south of US 24 on Saturday. The ECMWF \nhas the trough at 500mb slightly more amplified and further east by \nSaturday afternoon than the GFS and NAM do so that will make a \ndifference too as to where and when the highest PoPs occur. Severe \nweather is not expected at this time on Saturday.\n\nZonal flow continues aloft as yet another upper level ridge \nbuilds across the central CONUS, so have maintained warmer than \nnormal temperatures and low chances (20%) for PoPs through\nSunday and Monday. Since it is still several days out, there is\nstill a lot of uncertainty in how the overall synoptic pattern \nevolves early next week. Some models have a frontal system \ncoming through the Midwest and Upper Great Lakes Tuesday while \nothers keep the upper level ridge dominant over the area. There \nare no clear signs of fall in any of the long range guidance as \nour forecast area looks to remain sandwiched between hot and \nhumid air to our south and cooler, drier air to our north for \nthe foreseeable future.",
